WEBVTT

NOTE
Captions of "Glycolysis" by Drew Berry, wehi.tv, for HHMI BioInteractive, from the film's page on YouTube (youtu.be/BO0zL03CtDs).
Each cue is one line here, and mishearings in the captions are put right (plans/follow-the-molecule/captions.py lists them).

1
00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:04.290
The sweet, energetic glucose molecule

2
00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:09.540
is the energy source providing power for your living cells.

3
00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.750
Glucose, from the food you eat, is slowly

4
00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:16.680
broken down into two pyruvate molecules,

5
00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:22.380
extracting energy in a sequence of enzyme reactions called

6
00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:23.120
glycolysis.

7
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.520
The glycolysis pathway requires energy

8
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.510
to begin, delivered by chemical fuel, ATP.

9
00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:42.350
The primary source of ATP in your cells

10
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:44.630
are mitochondria organelles.

11
00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:51.570
Deep inside mitochondria, chemical reactions

12
00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:55.440
of aerobic respiration consume oxygen

13
00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:03.080
from the air you breathe to generate vast amounts of ATP.

14
00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:07.520
Sitting on this abundant supply of ATP

15
00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:12.900
is the first enzyme of the glycolysis pathway.

16
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:18.240
The reaction of the first enzyme transfers a phosphate group

17
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:23.940
from ATP to glucose, creating glucose 6-phosphate.

18
00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:42.220
The second enzyme rearranges glucose 6-phosphate

19
00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:45.760
into its isomer, fructose 6-phosphate.

20
00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:02.490
The third enzyme uses a second molecule of ATP,

21
00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:07.500
creating fructose 1, 6-bisphosphate.

22
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:11.310
The six-carbon sugar is now ready to be broken apart.

23
00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:25.040
The fourth enzyme cuts the molecule in half,

24
00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:28.160
creating two three-carbons sugars,

25
00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:32.840
each with a single phosphate attached.

26
00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:36.170
The sugar products are isomers of each other.

27
00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:39.230
But only glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate

28
00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:41.870
is ready to continue with glycolysis.

29
00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:56.280
The sugar isomer, dihydroxyacetone phosphate

30
00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:00.570
must first be converted through isomerization

31
00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:02.940
before continuing to the sixth enzyme.

32
00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:17.200
The sixth enzyme adds a second phosphate

33
00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:21.340
to glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate, creating 1,

34
00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:26.140
3 bisphosphoglycerate, while two electrons

35
00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:31.350
are transferred to NAD plus, which is reduced to NADH.

36
00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:37.720
Halfway through glycolysis, the cell

37
00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:43.210
has consumed two ATP molecules, breaking down glucose,

38
00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:46.630
and is now ready to capture energy in return.

39
00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:02.030
The seventh enzyme transfers a phosphate group

40
00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:07.070
from 1, 3-bisphosphoglycerate to ADP,

41
00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:09.830
creating chemical fuel, ATP.

42
00:04:21.779 --> 00:04:25.380
The eighth enzyme rearranges the phosphate

43
00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:27.960
from the end of the molecule to the middle.

44
00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:44.730
The ninth enzyme catalyzes dehydration,

45
00:04:44.730 --> 00:04:48.540
increasing the potential energy in preparation

46
00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:50.410
for the final glycolysis reaction.

47
00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:05.970
The 10th enzyme transfers the remaining phosphate group

48
00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:14.900
to ADP, producing ATP and the three-carbon sugar, pyruvate.

49
00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:19.940
Glycolysis finally produces a net gain in ATP.

50
00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:27.670
The glycolysis pathway breaks down glucose

51
00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:35.620
into two molecules of pyruvate, gaining two ATP and two NADH

52
00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:37.530
in the process.
